            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>Case Scene: 15 Accounts, $70K Monthly Spend, and a Team in Chaos</h2>
<p>A cross-border e-commerce team manages 15 advertising accounts with a monthly spend of approximately 500,000 RMB. Operations supervisor Xiao Li is overwhelmed:</p>
<ul>
<li>Each optimizer's accounts show wildly inconsistent performance—some achieve ROAS of 4.0 while others continuously lose money</li>
<li>Financial reconciliation never adds up: spending records don't match top-up records</li>
<li>Three accounts were suspended for ad policy violations, affecting other accounts under the same entity</li>
</ul>
<p>"Everyone is working hard, but why does account management keep getting messier?" Xiao Li's frustration represents the reality of many multi-account advertising teams. The problem isn't individual capability—it's the lack of a systematic account tiering mechanism.</p>
<h2>Problem Analysis: Three Dimensions for Account Classification</h2>
<p>The first step in account tiering is establishing clear criteria. We evaluate each account's value across three dimensions:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Evaluation Dimension</th>
<th>Content</th>
<th>Basis</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>Spend Volume</strong></td>
<td>Historical monthly average</td>
<td>&lt;50K / 50K-200K / 200K+</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Campaign Lifecycle</strong></td>
<td>Account stage</td>
<td>New product testing / Stable scaling</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Performance</strong></td>
<td>CAC and ROAS</td>
<td>Profitable / Break-even / Losing money</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Judgment Principle</strong>: Track each account's core metrics on a weekly basis to avoid decision-making based on single-day fluctuations.</p>
</blockquote>
<h2>Tiering Standards: A Three-Tier Account System</h2>
<p>Based on these dimensions, we classify accounts into three tiers:</p>
<h3>Tier 1: Core Accounts (60%-70% of total spend)</h3>
<p><strong>Characteristics</strong>: Stable, on-target ROI, mature account structure, primary revenue driver.</p>
<p><strong>Management Priority</strong>: Highest</p>
<ul>
<li>Prioritize top-up budget to avoid campaign interruption from insufficient balance</li>
<li>Daily balance monitoring with early warning alerts</li>
<li>Maintain stable creative strategy, test new directions with controlled budgets</li>
</ul>
<h3>Tier 2: Growth Accounts (20%-30% of total spend)</h3>
<p><strong>Characteristics</strong>: Fluctuating but non-loss ROI, at a critical optimization stage with potential to become core accounts.</p>
<p><strong>Management Priority</strong>: Medium-High</p>
<ul>
<li>Regular top-ups to maintain spend rhythm</li>
<li>Weekly performance reviews, adjust targeting or bidding</li>
<li>Focus on testing new creatives with rapid iteration</li>
</ul>
<h3>Tier 3: Test Accounts (~10% of total spend)</h3>
<p><strong>Characteristics</strong>: Insufficient data or unstable ROI, need to validate direction.</p>
<p><strong>Management Priority</strong>: Flexible</p>
<ul>
<li>Top-up on-demand with controlled budget caps</li>
<li>Periodic performance evaluation to decide upgrade or closure</li>
<li>Creative testing focused on validating conversion, not scaling</li>
</ul>
<h2>Action Items: Four Key Actions to Implement Tiering</h2>
<p>After establishing tiering standards, execution is critical. Here are four core action items:</p>
<h3>1. Differentiated Top-Up Budgets</h3>
<p>Different tiers have different capital requirements:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Core Accounts</strong>: Maintain 5-7 days of spend reserve; top-up response time under 2 hours</li>
<li><strong>Growth Accounts</strong>: Maintain 3-5 days of spend reserve; weekly fixed top-up day</li>
<li><strong>Test Accounts</strong>: Top-up on-demand; single top-up capped at 3 days of spend</li>
</ul>
<h3>2. Tiered Creative Management</h3>
<p>Creatives are the core asset in multi-account advertising, and tiered management improves efficiency:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Core Accounts</strong>: Prioritize validated, mature creatives; test new creatives with controlled budgets</li>
<li><strong>Growth Accounts</strong>: Test at least 2 new creative sets weekly; migrate successful creatives to core accounts</li>
<li><strong>Test Accounts</strong>: Focus on validating conversion, not chasing virality</li>
</ul>
<h3>3. Financial Reconciliation SOP</h3>
<p>Multi-account reconciliation is a pain point; tiering simplifies the process:</p>
<pre><code>Daily Actions:
├── Core Accounts: Export spend report → Compare with top-up records → Investigate discrepancies over 5%
├── Growth Accounts: Aggregate weekly spend → Verify weekly top-ups
└── Test Accounts: Monthly aggregation is sufficient
</code></pre>
<h3>4. Abnormal Account Upgrade/Downgrade Mechanism</h3>
<p>Account performance changes dynamically, requiring clear upgrade and downgrade rules:</p>
<p><strong>Downgrade Triggers</strong>:<br />
- ROAS below target by 30% for 2 consecutive weeks<br />
- Account balance consistently below safety threshold</p>
<p><strong>Upgrade Triggers</strong>:<br />
- ROAS exceeds target by 15% for 4 consecutive weeks<br />
- Single account monthly spend breaks into next tier threshold</p>
<h2>Results: From Chaos to Order</h2>
<p>Three months after implementing tiered management, Xiao Li's team saw significant improvements:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Metric</th>
<th>Before</th>
<th>After</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Financial reconciliation time</td>
<td>8 hours/week</td>
<td>2 hours/week</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Core account spend proportion</td>
<td>45%</td>
<td>65%</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Account suspension losses</td>
<td>2x/month</td>
<td>1x/quarter</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>More importantly, the team shifted from "putting out fires wherever they appear" to "strategically allocating resources." Core accounts received stable support, growth accounts had clear optimization paths, and test accounts no longer consumed excessive attention.</p>
<h2>Closing Thoughts</h2>
<p>Account tiering isn't a one-time task—it's an ongoing management habit. We recommend a monthly tier review to dynamically adjust based on performance data. Teams can also simplify tiering standards based on their scale—fewer than 5 accounts may only need two tiers.</p>
<p>There's only one core principle: <strong>Focus limited energy on the most valuable accounts.</strong></p>
